  <h1>How to learn Arabic: a beginner plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">Arabic looks challenging because of its script, but it is built on a very logical system: 28 letters, a root system where words grow from three-letter roots, and regular grammar. Learn the alphabet first, then sounds, then words — and Arabic starts to open up.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn the Arabic alphabet</h2>
  <p>Arabic has 28 letters, and each letter changes shape depending on whether it is at the start, middle or end of a word. That sounds hard, but the shapes follow clear patterns, and most learners can read the alphabet within two to three weeks of short daily practice.</p>
  <p>Learn to write each letter as you learn to read it — writing is the fastest way to remember the shapes. Arabic is written right to left, which feels strange for a day or two and then becomes natural.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn the sounds that are new to you</h2>
  <p>Arabic has sounds that do not exist in English, like the deep “h” (ح), the “ayn” (ع) and the emphatic consonants. Do not skip these — learn them one at a time, by listening and copying. Short vowels in Arabic are usually not written, so you will learn to read words from their shape and context, which comes with practice.</p>
  <p>Listen every day, even if you understand nothing yet. Your ear needs time to hear the difference between sounds that all seem similar at first.</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn the root system</h2>
  <p>Most Arabic words grow from a three-letter root. From the root k-t-b (writing), you get كتاب (kitaab, book), كاتب (kaatib, writer) and مكتبة (maktaba, library). Once you start noticing roots, new words become much easier to guess and remember.</p>
  <p>This is one of the best things about Arabic: learn one root and you get a whole family of words for free.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Learn core words and first sentences</h2>
  <p>Start with greetings (السلام عليكم — assalaamu alaykum, hello; شكراً — shukran, thank you; من فضلك — min fadlik, please), numbers, time, family, food and everyday verbs. Learn words inside sentences: “أنا طالب” (ana taalib — I am a student).</p>
  <p>Arabic sentences are often simpler than they look: there is no verb “to be” in the present tense, so “I am a student” is just “I student”. That makes your first sentences easier than you might expect.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Choose: Modern Standard Arabic or a dialect?</h2>
  <p>Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) is used in books, news and formal settings across the whole Arab world. Dialects — like Egyptian, Levantine or Gulf Arabic — are what people speak at home and in the street. Most learners start with MSA, because it is understood everywhere and all learning materials use it, then add a dialect later for everyday conversation.</p>
  <p>Tell your tutor which one you want to learn, so your lessons match your goal — travel, work, family or reading.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Skipping the alphabet and depending on transliteration — it blocks real reading.</li>
    <li>Trying to learn MSA and a dialect at the same time as a beginner.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the new sounds and hoping they will fix themselves.</li>
    <li>Learning words alone, without the sentences they belong in.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn the alphabet in your first 2–3 weeks — it unlocks everything.</li>
    <li>Write every new letter and word by hand.</li>
    <li>Learn roots, not just words — one root gives you a word family.</li>
    <li>Listen daily, even passively, to train your ear for new sounds.</li>
  </ul>

