  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn Arabic?</summary><p>Arabic takes longer than French or Spanish for English speakers, mainly because of the script and the new sounds. With 30 minutes a day, most learners can read the alphabet and handle simple greetings and sentences in 3–6 months, and hold basic conversations within a year or two. Steady daily practice matters more than speed.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Arabic hard to learn?</summary><p>It has hard parts — a new script, new sounds, and the difference between MSA and dialects. But it also has easy parts: the root system makes vocabulary logical, the grammar is regular, and there is no verb “to be” in the present tense. It is very learnable with a clear plan.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Should I learn Modern Standard Arabic or a dialect?</summary><p>Start with Modern Standard Arabic. It is understood everywhere, it is what books and courses use, and it gives you a base for any dialect later. Add a dialect when you know where and with whom you will speak Arabic.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn Arabic by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for the alphabet, vocabulary and grammar. Pronunciation of the new sounds and real conversation need a real person — a tutor can hear your sounds, correct them, and help you practise speaking.</p></details>
